      "rev_n_1_678ba1_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_678ba1_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_678ba1",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the parity of consecutive k cards",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794536363.6662152
      },
      "rev_n_2_1b4a96_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_1b4a96_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_1b4a96",
        "thought_text": "Consider the impact of flipping on future moves",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794536363.666235
      },
      "rev_n_3_e3f7cd_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_e3f7cd_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_e3f7cd",
        "thought_text": "Examine the relationship between n and k",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794536363.6662471
      },
      "rev_n_1_678ba1_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_678ba1_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_678ba1",
        "thought_text": "The current approach of analyzing the parity of consecutive k cards is incorrect. Instead, we should focus on the number of segments where the color side changes direction. This will give us a more accurate count of the moves required to make all color sides face up or down.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_678ba1_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_9298ddc1c0",
        "created_at": 1794536382.7673416
      },
      "rev_n_2_1b4a96_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_1b4a96_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_1b4a96",
        "thought_text": "The error in the previous reasoning was that it didn't account for the possibility of Quailty making optimal moves to prevent Tokitsukaze from winning. The current approach only considered the total number of moves, which doesn't always reflect the optimal play strategy. A better approach would be to consider the parity of the number of segments with alternating colors, as this directly affects the winning strategy.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_1b4a96_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_6268b1893b",
        "created_at": 1794536408.201744
      },
      "rev_n_3_e3f7cd_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_e3f7cd_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_e3f7cd",
        "thought_text": "The error in the code is in the condition for determining when Quailty wins. The current implementation checks if there are more than two segments, which is incorrect. Quailty can always win if there are exactly two segments, regardless of their lengths. The correct condition should be 'if len(segments) == 2:' instead of 'if len(segments) > 2:'. This oversight causes the function to incorrectly determine Quailty's win condition in cases where there are exactly two segments.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_e3f7cd_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_a33c05254d",
        "created_at": 1794536458.4790945
